<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      ============================================================
      IMPORTANT — READ BEFORE A DEVICE BUILD OR ARCHIVE
      ============================================================
      com.apple.developer.carplay-driving-navigation is a RESTRICTED
      entitlement. Apple only grants it after a developer submits a
      separate CarPlay entitlement request (from the Apple Developer
      account's "Additional Capabilities" request form) explaining the
      app's turn-by-turn navigation use case, and Apple approves it —
      this is an application/review process with Apple, not a checkbox
      you flip in Xcode or in this file. See docs/NAVIGATION.md for the
      request process and current status.

      Until that approval lands for this app's bundle ID
      (solutions.clearkey.timinow):
        - Xcode will refuse to create/download a provisioning profile
          that satisfies this entitlement for a real device or archive
          build.
        - Simulator builds with CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO (as used in CI —
          see .github/workflows/native-clients.yml) are unaffected, since
          entitlement enforcement happens at code-signing time.
        - iOS never instantiates CarPlaySceneDelegate at all without this
          grant, regardless of what Info.plist declares.
      ============================================================
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.carplay-driving-navigation</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      Native push (APNs) for the "a clinic answered" moment — the phone-side
      half of the Worker's sendApnsPush (src/push.js). "production" is right
      for every build that leaves this machine: a TestFlight or App Store
      archive (scripts/upload-testflight.sh), and a device install signed
      with a distribution profile.

      A Debug build run straight from Xcode on a development-signed device is
      the one case this does not cover — a development-signed app must carry
      "development" here to receive push at all, and the server side has to
      agree (APNS_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox in that deployment's .env, see
      .env.example). Xcode's automatic signing does not rewrite this value
      for you the way it does for the CarPlay entitlement's provisioning
      profile match, so testing sandbox push locally means editing this key
      to "development" on your own machine — never commit that edit, the
      same rule scripts/install-ios-device.sh's TimiNow.local.entitlements
      already follows for CarPlay.
    -->
    <key>aps-environment</key>
    <string>production</string>
</dict>
</plist>
